partition_graph="
A graph of the partition relationships would look like this: 

	            Paragon(TM) System
        ______________________________________________
       | node_set | node_set | node_set | node_set |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |----------|
       |          |          |          | node_set |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |          |          |          |          |
       |__________|__________|__________|__________|
                       
                  |---- node_group -----|

A 'node_set' is a rectangular collection of nodes defined with the
'node_set' configuration parameter. A node_group is a collection of
one or more node sets, defined with the 'node_group' configuration
parameter. Queues are assigned to a node_group with the QMGR SEt NODE_Group
subcommand.
"
#########
time_zone_help="
The 'time_zone' parameter indicates which time zone the 'prime_start' and
'prime_end' hour specifications are relative to.  The time zone is typically
set to the local time zone but can be set to any valid OSF/1 time zone
specification.
"
#############
prime_start_help="
The 'prime_start' and 'prime_end' parameters determine when NQS changes the
size of the batch partition under its control.  Both parameters are specified
as a series of 7 space-separated hours, in 24 hour time notation, beginning
with Sunday as the first field.
"
#############
prime_end_help=$prime_start_help
#############
cur_open_name_help="
During prime time, the partition named by open_p_name is active and the
partition named by open_np_name is inactive. During non-prime time, the
partition named by open_p_name is inactive and the partition named by
open_np_name is active.

When the time changes from prime time to non-prime time, NQS renames the
cur_open_name partition to the nane defined by open_p_name, and then
renames the name defined by open_np_name to cur_open_name. The reverse is
done when the time changes from non-prime time to prime time. Thus the
active open partition is always named by the value supplied by
cur_open_name. This is done so that users always see a single open
partition name in both operating modes.

If this feature is not desired, 'open_p_name', 'open_np_name', and
 'cur_open_name' should all be left blank.
"
#############
open_np_name_help=$cur_open_name_help
#############
open_p_name_help=$cur_open_name_help
#############
timeshare_help="
The 'timeshare' parameter is a boolean value that allows two jobs to share
nodes.  A value of 0 turns off job time sharing.  A value of 1 allows, when
possible, a second job in the time sharing layer.
"
#############
block_pri_help="
The 'block_pri' parameter is an integer value that defines the minimum 
dynamic priority a job must have in order for it to block other jobs not in 
the time sharing layer.  The scheduling scheme tends to favor smaller jobs.  
To allow a large job which has reached the top of the queue to be 
scheduled, jobs with a lower dynamic priority will not be scheduled if the 
dynamic priority of the large job is greater than the value specified in 
block_pri.  The default value is "-1", meaning no blocking will take place.  
A value of 0 for 'block_ts_pri' would mean all jobs can block a second job 
from being scheduled.
"
#############
timesh_pri_help="
The 'timesh_pri' parameter is an integer value that defines the minimum
dynamic priority required for a job to trigger time sharing with another job.
NQS fills the first scheduling layer without considering the minimum dynamic
priority requirement in order to prevent nodes from being idle.  In order for
a job to be scheduled in the time sharing layer, its dynamic priority must be
greater than or equal to the value specified in 'timesh_pri'.  For example, a
value of 0 allows all jobs in the time sharing layer.
"
#############
tsched_pri_help="
The 'tsched_pri' parameter is an integer value that defines the minimum NQS
queue priority required for a queue to become a dedicated job (TSCHED) queue.
Jobs submitted to a TSCHED queue using the -a switch of the qsub(1) command are
run at the time specified and are not be preempted by other jobs run by NQS.
"
#############
chk_runlimit_help="
The 'chk_runlimit' parameter is a boolean value that defines whether the NQS
complex and queue run limits should be checked before allowing a job to run.
In standard NQS implementations, the run limit determines the maximum number
of jobs that will be allowed to run in a queue or complex.  The enhancements
made to NQS for Paragon reduce the importance of these limits.  A value of
1 will enforce standard NQS run limits.  A value of 0 causes the run limits
to be ignored.
"
#############
age_factor_help="
The 'age_factor' parameter is a floating point value used to calculate the
dynamic priority of a job.  The dynamic priority is calculated using the
following equation:

     dynamic_priority = (nqs_queue_priority + (age_factor * time))

Time is a floating point term equal to the hours the job has been waiting in
the queue.
"
#############
grace_time_help="
The 'grace_time' parameter is an integer value specifying the number of
seconds NQS will wait between sending a SIGTERM and the SIGKILL signals.
When an NQS job exceeds the run time limit of the queue, NQS sends the job
a SIGTERM signal.  The job then has 'grace_time' seconds to before a SIGKILL
symbol is sent to the job.
"
#############
rollin_quan_help="
The 'rollin_quan' parameter is an integer value that specifies, in seconds,
the rollin quantum of NQS controlled partitions.  For best batch performance,
this value should not be less than 60 seconds.
"
#############
do_wall_help="
The 'do_wall' parameter is a boolean value that causes NQS to send a wall(1)
message when it switches between prime and non-prime time.  The message is
sent to all logged in users of `hostname`.  A value of 0 indicates that a
broadcast message is not sent.  A value of 1 indicates that a message is sent.
"
#############
node_set_help="
A 'node set' is  a rectangular collection of nodes defined by a unique set of 
physical nodes in the configuration file. The syntax is as follows :

        node_set<set_number>: <root_partition> <node_spec>

	where:
	 
	node_set is the configuration file parameter

	<set_number> is a numerical value (between 0 and 63) that identifies
	the node_set. 

	<root partition> is the partition under which the NQS daemon will 
        create partitions for the NQS jobs running in this 'node_set'.

	node_spec is the location and dimension of the 'node_set'. The format
	for 'node_spec' is heightxwidth:starting_node.  For example,
	8x32:0 specifies a rectangle of 256 nodes starting at parent partition 
        node zero.

For example: 

	node_set0: .compute.GP 8x8:0
	node_set1: .compute.GP 8x4:8
	node_set2: .compute.MP 8x1:12
	node_set3: .compute.MP 8x2:13
					 
These input lines define four 'node_sets'. 'Node_sets' 0 and 1 
have a common root partition--.compute.GP--while both 'node_set 2 and 3 
have root partition .compute.MP.
"
#############
node_group_help="
A 'node_group' consists of one or more 'node sets'. Each NQS batch 
queue is associated with a 'node_group' which defines the group of 'node 
sets' to use when scheduling jobs submitted to the queue.
 

A 'node_group' is  defined in the configuration file with the following syntax.

        node_group<group_number>: <first node_set> <second node_set> ...

	where:

	node_group is the configuration file parameter 

	<group_number> is a numerical value (between 0 and 63) that
	identifies the 'node_group' 

	<first node_set> <second node_set> are one or more 'node_set' numbers 
	that compose the 'node_group'.

For example, suppose we have four 'node_sets' with the following physical 
attributes :
	 
	node_set0 - 16 Mb GP nodes
	node_set1 - 32 Mb GP nodes
	node_set2 - 16 Mb MP nodes
	node_set3 - 32 Mb MP nodes
	
and we define four 'node_groups' in the configuration file as follows :

	node_group0:0 1
	node_group1:1
	node_group2:2 3
	node_group3:3

'Node_group' 0 includes both the 16 and 32 Mb GP nodes and 'Node_group' 2 
includes both the 16 and 32 Mb MP nodes. 'Node_group' 1 consists of only 
the 32 Mb GP nodes and 'Node_group' 3 consists of only the 32 Mb MP nodes.

Therefore, 'Node_group' 0 can be used to characterize requests that would run
in any GP nodes, regardless of the memory size, while requests associated
with 'Node_group' 1 will only run in 32 Mb GP nodes. 'Node_groups' 2 and 3 
have similar implication except they are for the MP nodes.
"
#############
prime_list_help="
The prime_list configuration parameter defines the node_sets within 
a node_group that are used during prime time.  
The syntax for prime_list is as follows.

        prime_list<node_group>: <first node_set> <second node_set> ...

For example:

	prime_list0 : 1 2
"
############# 
nprime_list_help=" 


The nprime_list configuration parameter defines the node_sets within 
a node_group that are used during non-prime time.  
The syntax for prime_list is as follows.

        nprime_list<node_group>: <first node_set> <second node_set> ...

For example:

	nprime_list1 : 2 3
" 
############# 
prompt=" 
Please specify or validate the NQS scheduling parameters given in the following
prompts.  The values displayed in brackets are the default values.  To use the 
defaults, just press the Return key.  If you want to change the default or 
when no default is given, enter the desired value followed by the Return 
key.  If you want more information on a parameter, enter a question mark 
(?).  "
############
np_overrun_help="
The np_overrun configuration parameter determines if NQS will schedule
jobs to run during non-prime time if the requested time plus the current
time will overrun into prime time. If this parameter is set to 0, jobs
can overrun into Prime time. If it is set to 1, jobs cannot overrun. 
The default value is 0.
"
############
macs_flag_help="
The macs_flag configuration parameter determines if NQS will communicate 
with MACS. This parameter allows MACS to distiguish between NQS batch jobs
and interactive jobs. If MACS is running and macs_flag is set to 0, all NQS 
jobs reported in the MACS reports appear as interactive jobs. If MACS is 
running and macs_flag is set to 1, NQS jobs are differentiated from 
interactive jobs. If MACS is not running and macs_flag is set to 1, all NQS
jobs will fail.
The default value is 0.
"
############
use_login_help="
The 'use_login' parameter is a boolean value that tells NQS whether or not
to spawn the user's shell as a login shell.  A value of 1 causes NQS to
spawn the user's shell as a login shell, which makes the shell read the
user's .profile or .login file as it starts up (this is done by invoking
the shell with a dash prepended to its name: '-sh', '-csh', or '-ksh').  A
value of 0 causes NQS to spawn the user's shell as a non-login shell, which
means that the shell does NOT read the user's .profile or .login file.
The default value is 0.
"
############
nosched_help="
The 'nosched' parameter is a boolean value that tells NQS whether or not to
schedule jobs.  A value of 1 causes NQS to not schedule any jobs at all.  A
value of 0 causes NQS to schedule jobs normally.

This parameter is almost always set to 0 during normal operation.  A
typical use of this parameter is to prevent NQS from re-scheduling a job
that crashed the system and will start again when the system comes up.  If
this occurs, use the following procedure to prevent this job from running:

    1. Boot the system to single-user mode.
    2. Edit the sched_param file and change 'nosched' to 1.
    3. Continue booting to multi-user mode.
    4. Remove the problem job from the queue.
    5. Edit the sched_param file and change 'nosched' to 0.
    6. Use the 'qstart' command to force NQS to re-read the 
       sched_param file.

The default value is 0.
"
############
prime_script_help="
The 'prime_script' parameter specifies the full pathname of an
executable file (typically a shell script) which is run at the
beginning of prime time.  The script is run after all other processing
has been performed for the non-prime/prime switchover.

NOTE: the specified file is executed as root.  If it is a shell script,
it needs to be properly protected from unauthorized modification.

The default value is blank (meaning that no script is executed).
"
############
nprime_script_help="
The 'nprime_script' parameter specifies the full pathname of an
executable file (typically a shell script) which is run at the
beginning of non-prime time.  The script is run after all other
processing has been performed for the prime/non-prime switchover.

NOTE: the specified file is executed as root.  If it is a shell script,
it needs to be properly protected from unauthorized modification.

The default value is blank (meaning that no script is executed).
"
############
wallclock_limits_help="
The 'wallclock_limits' parameter is a boolean value that determines how
NQS enforces per-request CPU time limits.

A value of 1 causes CPU time limits to be enforced by strict 'wall
clock' time.  For example, a limit of 30 minutes means that a job can
run no longer than 30 minutes as measured by the clock on the wall of
the computer room (even if it spends part or all of the time
gang-scheduled out).

A value of 0 causes CPU time limits to be enforced by node-hours.  In
this case, the CPU time limit is multipled by the number of nodes in
the job to give the maximum number of node-hours the job is allowed to
use.  For example, a job run in a 16-node queue (with no -lP switch)
with a limit of 30 minutes can use no more than 8 node-hours total.
This means that jobs that do not use all the nodes that have been
allocated to them (by use of the -sz switch within the job script) can
run longer than the limit.

The default value is 0.
"
